  "turns": [
    {
      "turn": 1,
      "doctor_question": "Hello, please have a seat. What brings you in to see me today?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S022",
      "patient_response": "Well, my lieutenant... he's the one who made me come down here. I didn't even think I needed to leave the station, to be honest with you. See, I was just in the apparatus bay, right by the rigs. I'm an EMT, so I know the gear, I know how things are supposed to be. But I was looking at the airway bags, and they just... they weren't right. So I started moving them. I kept moving the airway bags into this pattern, because I just knew, I knew it was the exact right way they had to sit. It’s like a grid, almost, and it had to be perfect. And while I was doing it, I was repeating the dispatch numbers out loud, just saying them over and over so the rhythm matched the way I was setting the bags down. \n\nThen my lieutenant comes walking up, and he's telling me to stop. He's saying to step away from the bags, step away from the rig, but I couldn't. I wouldn't step away. He didn't understand what was happening, and he started getting kind of loud about it. I tried to make him see that I couldn't just stop in the middle of it. If I broke the sequence right then, it felt like the whole bay would go bad. Like the concrete, the trucks, the air, the whole station—everything would just go completely bad if I didn't finish the pattern with the numbers. But he wouldn't listen to me, he just pulled me off shift and told me I had to come see you.",
