    {
      "turn": 3,
      "doctor_question": "During these past two months, have you felt like you need much less sleep than usual to feel rested and energized?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Sleep? No, I wouldn't say I feel energized. I mean, I'm definitely sleeping, but it's not like I'm bouncing off the walls or anything. I just... I try to get my rest when I can, you know? But the thing is, even when I'm lying there in my bunk at the station, or at home in my own bed, my mind is still going over the grid. The patterns. I can close my eyes, but I still see the airway bags, and I still hear the dispatch numbers ticking away in my head like a metronome. Tick, tick, tick, one-four-niner, two-seven-six. So, I do sleep, I get my hours in, mostly, but the resting part? I don't know if you could call it restful because the responsibility doesn't stop just because I close my eyes. If I'm asleep, who's making sure the frequencies from the cameras aren't bleeding into the incident logs? Nobody, that's who. So I wake up and I feel this weight, this heaviness, like I've been working a double shift just trying to keep the station from falling apart while I was unconscious. But no, I don't feel energized on less sleep, I just feel like there's never enough time to get everything aligned the way it absolutely has to be.",
