    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "Do you ever hear voices or sounds that other people can't hear when no one is around?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Voices? No, no, I don't hear people talking to me when nobody's there. I mean, I told you about the dispatch numbers, right? I hear those, but that's me, that's my own head keeping the rhythm, keeping the sequence going. It's not like some stranger whispering in the corner of the room or anything like that. But, you know, the sounds... the frequencies I mentioned before with the cameras? That's a different thing entirely. It's not a voice, it's more like a... like a hum. A low, vibrating hum that just gets under my skin. \n\nI first noticed it when I was trying to align the run reports. I was sitting at the desk, making sure the edges were perfectly parallel, and I just felt this vibration in my teeth. It was coming from the security camera up in the corner of the dayroom. And nobody else seemed to notice! The other guys were just watching TV, eating dinner, totally oblivious to the fact that this camera was putting out this... this interference. It's not a voice, but it's a sound, a frequency that messes with the alignment of everything. And it's not just the cameras, sometimes it's the fluorescent lights in the bay, or the radio static when it's just sitting there on the charger. It's like they're all humming at this wrong pitch, and if I don't correct the pattern, if I don't move the bags or stack the papers right, that hum is going to just shatter everything. But actual voices? Like someone saying, 'Hey, do this' or 'Do that'? No, I don't get that. It's just the hum, the frequencies, and the dispatch numbers that I have to repeat to keep the hum from taking over the station.",
